                        "form": "lovecraftienne"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "685f2c33-2356-41a9-8ecc-c340d306ee96",
                        "entry_id": "b17ac118-beac-34e3-ac03-8c9883712bb8",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "lovecraftiennes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0fe2ca8c-201f-3d77-9a28-facbe3cbe5cb",
                        "entry_id": "b17ac118-beac-34e3-ac03-8c9883712bb8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui appartient, s'inspire, ou ressemble aux espaces imaginaires, à l’œuvre, de l’écrivain américain Howard Phillips Lovecraft."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "1eceba6c-7b38-48fd-8798-4844148bcd3a",
                                "sense_id": "0fe2ca8c-201f-3d77-9a28-facbe3cbe5cb",
                                "text": "Dans l’univers lovecraftien, il faut réserver une place spéciale aux perceptions auditives."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "47d285c0-c68c-4a66-95fc-9e99ecce3c3f",
                                "sense_id": "0fe2ca8c-201f-3d77-9a28-facbe3cbe5cb",
                                "text": "En 1981, L’Appel de Cthulu, jeu de rôle culte, a commencé à répandre l’imaginaire lovecraftien, inspiré de la nouvelle publiée en 1928."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ee2f8e4e-2c84-4e18-ab94-f46cb3c84200",
                        "source_entry_id": "b17ac118-beac-34e3-ac03-8c9883712bb8",
                        "source_sense_id": "0fe2ca8c-201f-3d77-9a28-facbe3cbe5cb",
                        "target_lang_word": "Lovecraftian",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Lovecraftian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f8c78044-8767-3e2e-ab96-7f6349d7dc8d",
                                        "entry_id": "71fd7ce9-74f5-39b9-b920-4f7555e595d7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Frighteningly monstrous and otherworldly, sometimes with terrifyingly unnatural anatomy."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "99ba75eb-4bff-4134-8643-45b3020078c1",
                                                "sense_id": "f8c78044-8767-3e2e-ab96-7f6349d7dc8d",
                                                "text": "The tip of the thing was equipped with long whiplike appendages that writhed around a loose, drooling, toothless mouth large enough to swallow a man whole...Perhaps this was the only thing that the escaping Lovecraftian entity had thus far been able to extrude between the opening Gates — this one finger."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fee22c16-352c-3f26-a764-c37ed44fdde7",
                                        "entry_id": "71fd7ce9-74f5-39b9-b920-4f7555e595d7",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of, pertaining to, or emulating the style or works of author H. P. Lovecraft (1890–1937)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3e611b3c-4034-4ba9-9094-290bf914af73",
                                                "sense_id": "fee22c16-352c-3f26-a764-c37ed44fdde7",
                                                "text": "Lovecraftian horror"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a896f710-66f4-4da8-b929-a90a5d4312be",
                                                "sense_id": "fee22c16-352c-3f26-a764-c37ed44fdde7",
                                                "text": "Lovecraftian fiction"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e63597c4-a1f9-4bb7-a42f-8638c36386c9",
                                                "sense_id": "fee22c16-352c-3f26-a764-c37ed44fdde7",
                                                "text": "The present story, \"Born of the Winds\", is one of the best. For one thing, the vision of the story is pure Lovecraftian cosmic pessimism."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bed9019e-57ba-4e5a-8d65-84e2b587822b",
                                                "sense_id": "fee22c16-352c-3f26-a764-c37ed44fdde7",
                                                "text": "That abandonment, incidentally, compelled Sam’s mother to fill her young son’s head with H. P. Lovecraftian horror-style lies about the interior of Emily Dickinson’s house, lies that motivated her son’s break-in to begin with."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Lovecraftian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                        "entry_id": "02ed68a8-bbec-312b-8db2-8c76187a8f77",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A fan of American author H. P. Lovecraft (1890–1937)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "81e524d9-338b-4ef3-aa3c-d2be6f111692",
                                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                                "text": "For zealous Lovecraftians there are a few choice tidbits—a short autobiography, his commonplace book, and his “History and Chronology of the Necronomicon.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7b1e2f51-c7ce-49fe-9ab8-d45508818f7c",
                                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                                "text": "Finally, we can draw a definitive fourth circle, at the absolute heart of HPL’s myth, which contains what most rabid Lovecraftians continue to call, almost in spite of themselves, the “great texts.”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8d05a134-2e69-412c-ac26-7337a3f82987",
                                                "sense_id": "280c480f-b387-3e64-adf3-46eed499f4fa",
                                                "text": "Everyone is a “creative anachronist,” but we Lovecraftians, like our cousins in other Buddha-fields of fandom, have elected to live a minority, sectarian existence in what sociologists Berger and Luckmann (The Social Construction of Reality) call a “finite province of meaning.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "68120b30-f5eb-4e6e-9e75-6d1ef12f31a0",
